<?xml version="1.0"?>
<robot name="plugin_camera_common" xmlns:xacro="http://www.ros.org/wiki/xacro" xmlns:gz="http://gazebosim.org/schema">
  <!--
  THIS MACRO IMPLEMENTS THE COMMON PART OF ANY CAMERA ELEMENT FOR GAZEBO.
  THIS MACRO IS INTENDED TO BE USED INSIDE OTHER CAMERA MACROS, FOR EXAMPLE (PLUGIN_DEPTH_CAMERA_MACRO.XACRO, ETC.,),
  NOT ON ITS OWN.

  This macro can be used from sdformat 1.9 (possible even earlier) onwards: i.e, it can be used from Gazebo Fortress
  (officially bonded with ROS2 Humble) and later versions of Gazebo.

  The sdformat specifications for a camera sensor found at:
  http://sdformat.org/spec?ver=1.11&elem=sensor#sensor_camera (for version 1.11)

  sdformat specification 1.11 is parsed by the library libsdformat14, available since Gazebo Harmonic.

  Check the version of libsdformat installed on your system with the commands:
  ❯ gz sdf \-\-versions
    14.8.0

  or

  ❯ dpkg -l | grep sdformat
    ii  libsdformat12:amd64        12.8.0-1~jammy                 amd64   Simulation Description Format ...
    ii  libsdformat12-dev:amd64    12.8.0-1~jammy                 amd64   Simulation Description Format ...
    ii  libsdformat14:amd64        14.8.0-1~jammy                 amd64   Simulation Description Format ...
    ii  libsdformat14-dev:amd64    14.8.0-1~jammy                 amd64   Simulation Description Format ...
    ii  python3-sdformat14         14.8.0-1~jammy                 amd64   Simulation Description Format ...
    ii  ros-humble-sdformat-urdf   1.0.1-1jammy.20250823.081540   amd64   URDF plugin to parse SDFormat ...
    ii  sdformat12-sdf             12.8.0-1~jammy                 all     Simulation Description Format ...
    ii  sdformat14-sdf             14.8.0-1~jammy                 all     Simulation Description Format ...

  If you execute in the terminal:
  ❯ gz sdf -h
    Utilities for SDF files.

    gz sdf [options]

    Options:

      -k [ \-\-check ] arg                Check if an SDFormat file is valid.
      -d [ \-\-describe ] [SPEC VERSION]  Print the aggregated SDFormat spec description. Default version (1.11).
                                                                                                          ******

  Default version is 1.11, which means you can write sdf files with sdformat 1.11 and lower versions.

  Finally, if you execute in a terminal:
  ❯ gz sdf -d 1.11 | head

  you will see the beginning of the sdformat 1.11 specification in XML format, starting with:
  <element name ='sdf' required ='1'>
    <description><![CDATA[SDFormat base element that can include one model, actor, light, or worlds. A user of multiple worlds could run parallel instances of simulation, or offer selection of a world at runtime.]]></description>
    <attribute name ='version' type ='string' default ='1.10' required ='1'>
      <description><![CDATA[Version number of the SDFormat specification, consisting of major and minor versions delimited by a `.` character. A major version bump is required if older versions cannot be automatically converted to this version. A minor version bump is required when there are breaking changes that can be handled by the automatic conversion functionality encoded in the `*.convert` files.]]></description>
    </attribute>
  ....

  You can repeat the same for other versions like 1.10, 1.9, etc.

  At the moment of writing this macro (November 2025), where sdformat 1.12 is not yet available in Gazebo, if you
  execute:

  ❯ gz sdf -d 1.12 | head

   Error [Utils.cc:176] Unable to find SDF filename[root.sdf] with version 1.12Error [Utils.cc:176] Could not find the
   'element' element in the xml fileError: SDF schema initialization failed.

  With this macro plugin you can create any type of camera sensor in GZ.

  Depending on the values of some parameters, some fields will be omitted when expanding the macro.

  Convention for parameter types:
  R means real number (float or double)
  I means integer
  S means string
  B means boolean
  When no unit suffix is specified, the proper units assumed by ROS/Gazebo are used.
  When a different unit from what ROS/Gazebo assumes is used, it will be indicated properly with a suffix, like, _deg,
  to indicate degrees instead of radians (which is the default unit for angles in ROS/Gazebo).

  Parameter formats:
  name (S): A unique name for the camera.

  optical_frame (S): Name of the optical frame of the camera. It will be used in the camera_info message header
                     For example: color_optical_frame.

  save (S): Enable or disable saving of camera frames.
      save = 'enabled (B) path (S)'
      enabled (B): If true, saving of camera frames is enabled.
      path (S): The path name which will hold the frame data. If path name is relative, then directory is relative to
                current working directory.

  triggered (B): If true, the camera is triggered by a topic.

  trigger_topic (S): Name of the topic that will trigger the camera if enabled.

  info_topic (aka camera_info_topic) (S): Name of the camera info topic.

  visible_mask (I): Visibility mask of a camera. Bitmask filter that controls which visuals this camera will render.
                    A visual is visible if (visibility_mask & visual.visibility_flags) != 0.
                    A mask 0xFFFFFFFF typically means 'see everything'

  clip (S): The near and far clip planes used in simulation. Objects closer or farther than these planes are not
            rendered (captured by Gazebo in the image).
      clip = 'near (R) far (R)'
      near (R): Near clipping distance of the view frustum.
      far (R): Far clipping distance of the view frustum.

HOW TO CHOOSE OPTICAL PARAMETERS FOR STANDARD GNOMONICAL (PINHOLE) CAMERAS:
===========================================================================
For best realism, set lens intrinsics and distortion from your camera calibration (or manufacturer specs), so simulation
matches the real sensor.

If you only need a simple, ideal camera, you can assume a gnomonic (aka pinhole) lens, and in this case choosing
image_width_in_pixels, image_height_in_pixels, and horizontal_fov is enough to derive other optical parameters as
follows:
  - Choose image_width_in_pixels and image_height_in_pixels to be passed to the macro in the 'image' parameter.

  - Consider a horizontal_fov (ideal, undistorted) and compute:
      fx = (image_width_in_pixels * 0.5) / tan(horizontal_fov * 0.5)

  - Pass fx in the lens_intrinsics.fx parameter.

  - If you consider square pixels, which is the common case, then fy = fx.
    FYI, when pixels are square the following equality holds true:
      image_width_in_pixels/image_height_in_pixels = tan(0.5 * horizontal_fov)/tan(0.5 * vertical_fov).

    Therefore, it is easy to derive that fy = fx and the vertical_fov can be computed as:
      vertical_fov = 2 * atan( (image_height_in_pixels / image_width_in_pixels) * tan(0.5 * horizontal_fov) )

  - If you do not consider square pixels for some reason, then you first consider a vertical_fov independently and
    compute:
      fy = (image_height_in_pixels * 0.5) / tan(vertical_fov * 0.5)

    In this case:
      image_width_in_pixels/image_height_in_pixels != tan(0.5 * horizontal_fov)/tan(0.5 * vertical_fov)

    and fx and fy are computed independently and can be different.

  - Pass fy in the lens_intrinsics.fy parameter.

  - Compute cx (aka ppx: principal point x) and cy (aka ppy: principal point y) as:
      cx (ppx) = (image_width_in_pixels - 1) * 0.5
      cy (ppy) = (image_height_in_pixels - 1) * 0.5

      Why -1 in the formulas? Because pixel indices start at 0.
      Consider an image of width 640 pixels; pixels are indexed from 0 to 639. The first half of the image width
      contains pixels 0 to 319, and the second half contains pixels 320 to 639. Therefore, the center is at position
      319.5 in pixels, which is (640-1)/2 = 319.5

  - Pass cx and cy in the lens_intrinsics.cx and lens_intrinsics.cy parameters.

  - Compute center_x_norm and center_y_norm as:
      center_x_norm = (cx + 0.5) / image_width_in_pixels
      center_y_norm = (cy + 0.5) / image_height_in_pixels

      Why +0.5 in the formulas?
      We normalize distortion center in edge coordinates where the left border is 0 and right border is W, hence the
      +0.5 shift from pixel-center coordinates.

  - Pass center_x_norm and center_y_norm in the distortion.center_x and distortion.center_y parameters.

  - If you know the distortion coefficients of your camera lens (k1, k2, k3, p1, p2) set them in the distortion
    parameters. If you want an ideal pinhole camera without distortion, set all distortion coefficients to zero.

  image (S): The image size in pixels and format
      image = 'width (I) height (I) format (S) anti_aliasing (I)'
      width (I): Width of the image in pixels.
      height (I): Height of the image in pixels.
      format (S) = L8 | L16 | R8G8B8 | B8G8R8 | BAYER_RGGB8 | BAYER_BGGR8 | BAYER_GBRG8 | BAYER_GRBG8 |
                   | R_FLOAT16 | R_FLOAT32 (These last two formats are hardly used)
          The <format> field describes the format used for the data published in the color image message
          ${topic}/image.
          It is also the format Gazebo uses to render the color image in the GUI when <visualize> is enabled in the
          sensor.
          If you choose R8G8B8 you publish an RGB image, if you choose L8 you publish a monochrome image, etc.
          Float formats (R_FLOAT16 / R_FLOAT32) in <image><format> denote single-channel floating-point image formats,
          where each pixel stores a continuous scalar value (not RGB). Use them for HDR/physical magnitude maps (e.g.,
          intensity, radiance, scientific data); FLOAT16 is lighter, FLOAT32 more precise. Therefore, their use in
          <image><format> is problably limited, but they are supported if you have a specific use case.

      anti_aliasing (I): Value used for anti-aliasing.

  lens_single_elements (S): Single element lens (Single in the sense that these element are not composed by multiple
                            sub-elements as in lens_intrinsics, for example).
      lens_single_elements (S) = 'type (S) scale_to_hfov (B) cutoff_angle_deg (R) env_texture_size (I)'
      type (S): Type of the lens mapping. Supported values are: gnomonical, stereographic, equidistant, equisolid_angle,
                orthographic, custom.
                - gnomonical (pinhole): Standard perspective projection. Straight lines in space are projected as
                  straight lines in the image. Used in conventional cameras.
                  For gnomonical type it is recommended to specify a horizontal_fov <= 90°.
                - stereographic: Projection that preserves angles, common in fisheye lenses. Areas are distorted, but
                  angles remain unchanged.
                - equidistant: The radial distance in the image is proportional to the angle of incidence. Used in some
                  fisheye lenses.
                - equisolid_angle: Projection where the area in the image is proportional to the solid angle. Used in
                  fisheye lenses for scientific applications.
                - orthographic: Orthographic projection, without perspective. Objects retain their size regardless of
                  distance.
                - custom: Allows defining a custom angular mapping function using the custom_function field.
                  If custom is selected, you must also define the 'lens_custom_function' parameter with the appropriate
                  mapping function and constants (c1, c2, c3, f, fun).
      scale_to_hfov (B): If true the image will be scaled to fit horizontal FOV, otherwise it will be shown according to
                         projection type parameters.
      cutoff_angle_deg (R): Everything outside of the specified angle will be hidden, 90° by default.
      env_texture_size (I): Resolution of the environment cube map used to draw the world.
                            The highter it is, the sharper is your image.

  lens_custom_function (S): Definition of custom mapping function in a form of r=c1*f*fun(theta/c2 + c3).
                            See https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Fisheye_lens#Mapping_function
                            THE VALUES IN LENS_CUSTOM_FUNCTION ARE ONLY CONSIDERED IF 'TYPE' IN LENS_SINGLE_ELEMENTS IS
                            SET TO 'CUSTOM', OTHERWISE THEY ARE IGNORED.
      lens_custom_function (S) = 'c1 (R) c2 (R) c3 (R) f (R) fun (S)'
      c1 (R): Linear scaling constant.
      c2 (R): Angle scaling constant.
      c3 (R): Angle offset constant.
      f (R): Focal length of the optical system. Note: It's not a focal length of the lens in a common sense!
             This value is ignored if 'scale_to_fov' is set to true
      fun (S): Mapping function. Supported values are: 'tan', 'sin' and 'id'.

  lens_intrinsics (S): Camera intrinsic parameters for setting a custom perspective projection matrix.
      lens_intrinsics (S) = 'fx (R) fy (R) cx (R) cy (R) s (R)'
      fx (R): X focal length in pixels. A decimal part is allowed.
      fy (R): Y focal length in pixels. A decimal part is allowed.
      cx (R): principal point x in pixels. A decimal part is allowed.
      cy (R): principal point y in pixels. A decimal part is allowed.

    The intrinsic parameters above form the camera matrix K:

        K = | fx  s  cx |
            |  0 fy  cy |
            |  0  0   1 |
    where s is the 'lens_xy_skew' parameter.

  lens_xy_skew (R): Parameter in the camera intrinsic matrix that represents the tilt between the X and Y axes of the
                    image sensor. In an ideal sensor, pixels are perfectly aligned in rows and columns, and the X and Y
                    axes are perpendicular, so lens_xy_skew = 0.0. If the sensor is slightly deformed or the pixels are
                    not perfectly aligned, there may be a small tilt between the axes, and s would have a nonzero value.
                    In practice, almost all modern cameras have lens_xy_skew = 0.0, and only in special cases or
                    defective sensors is a different value used.

  lens_projection (S): Camera projection matrix P for overriding camera intrinsic matrix K values so that users can
                       configure P independently of K. This is useful when working with stereo cameras where P may be
                       different from K due to the transform between the two cameras.
      lens_projection (S) = 'p_fx p_fy p_cx p_cy tx ty'
      p_fx (R): X focal length for projection matrix, in pixels. It overrides fx. A decimal part is allowed.
      p_fy (R): Y focal length for projection matrix, in pixels. It overrides fy. A decimal part is allowed.
      p_cx (R): X principal point for projection matrix, in pixels. It overrides cx. A decimal part is allowed.
      p_cy (R): Y principal point for projection matrix, in pixels. It overrides cy. A decimal part is allowed.
      tx (R): X translation for projection matrix, in pixels. A decimal part is allowed.
      ty (R): Y translation for projection matrix, in pixels. A decimal part is allowed.

  distortion_k_p (S): Lens distortion to be applied to camera images.
                  See http://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Distortion_(optics)#Software_correction
      distortion (S) = 'k1 (R) k2 (R) k3 (R) p1 (R) p2 (R) center_x (R) center_y (R)'
      k1 (R): The radial distortion coefficient k1 (Default: 0.0).
      k2 (R): The radial distortion coefficient k2 (Default: 0.0).
      k3 (R): The radial distortion coefficient k3 (Default: 0.0).
      p1 (R): The tangential distortion coefficient p1 (Default: 0.0).
      p2 (R): The tangential distortion coefficient p2 (Default: 0.0).

    If the distortion parameters k1, k2, k3 (radial) and p1, p2 (tangential) are set to zero (their default values),
    no optical distortion will be applied to the camera image. The result is an ideal image, free from radial or
    tangential deformation, as if the lens were a perfect pinhole model without imperfections. This is the standard
    setting for most simulations where realistic lens distortion is not required.

  distortion_center (S): The normalized coordinates of the distortion center or principal point.
      distortion_center (S) = 'center_x_norm (R) center_y_norm (R)'
      center_x_norm (R): The normalized x coordinate of the distortion center or principal point, in the range
                         [0.0, 1.0].
      center_y_norm (R): The normalized y coordinate of the distortion center or principal point, in the range
                         [0.0, 1.0].

  gaussian_noise (S): The properties of the noise model that should be applied to generated images.
      gaussian_noise = 'mean (R) stddev (R)'
      mean (R): Mean of the Gaussian noise.
      stddev (R): Standard deviation of the Gaussian noise.
  -->
  <xacro:macro name="plugin_camera_common"
               params="name
                       optical_frame
                       save:='False /tmp'
                       triggered:=False
                       trigger_topic:=''
                       info_topic
                       visible_mask:=4294967295
                       clip:='0.1 100.0'
                       image
                       lens_single_elements:='gnomonical true 90.0 256'
                       lens_custom_function:=''
                       lens_intrinsics:=''
                       lens_projection:=''
                       lens_xy_skew:=0.0
                       distortion_k_p:=''
                       distortion_center:='0.5 0.5'
                       gaussian_noise:='0.0 0.0'">

    <!-- <topic>
          ${node_namespace}/${node_name}/color/image_raw
          <camera_info_topic>${node_namespace}/${node_name}/color/camera_info</camera_info_topic>
        </topic> -->

    <xacro:property name="save_seq"                 value="${save.split()}"/>

    <xacro:if value="${len(save_seq) != 2}">
      ${xacro.fatal("Invalid save parameter format. Expected format: 'enabled (B) path (S)'")}
    </xacro:if>

    <xacro:property name="save_enabled"             value="${save_seq[0]}"/>
    <xacro:property name="save_path"                value="${save_seq[1]}"/>

    <xacro:property name="clip_seq"                 value="${clip.split()}"/>

    <xacro:if value="${len(clip_seq) != 2}">
      ${xacro.fatal("Invalid clip parameter format. Expected format: 'near (R) far (R)'")}
    </xacro:if>

    <xacro:property name="near_clip"                value="${clip_seq[0]}"/>
    <xacro:property name="far_clip"                 value="${clip_seq[1]}"/>
    <xacro:property name="image_seq"                value="${image.split()}"/>

    <xacro:if value="${len(image_seq) != 4}">
      ${xacro.fatal("Invalid image parameter format. Expected format: 'width (I) height (I) format (S) anti_aliasing (I)'")}
    </xacro:if>

    <xacro:property name="image_width_px"           value="${image_seq[0]}"/>
    <xacro:property name="image_height_px"          value="${image_seq[1]}"/>
    <xacro:property name="image_format"             value="${image_seq[2]}"/>
    <xacro:property name="image_anti_aliasing"      value="${image_seq[3]}"/>

    <xacro:property name="lens_single_elements_seq" value="${lens_single_elements.split()}"/>

    <xacro:if value="${len(lens_single_elements_seq) != 4}">
      ${xacro.fatal("Invalid lens_single_elements parameter format. Expected format: 'type (S) scale_to_hfov (B) cutoff_angle_deg (R) env_texture_size (I)'")}
    </xacro:if>

    <xacro:property name="lens_type"                value="${lens_single_elements_seq[0]}"/>
    <xacro:property name="lens_scale_to_hfov"       value="${lens_single_elements_seq[1]}"/>
    <xacro:property name="lens_cutoff_angle_deg"    value="${lens_single_elements_seq[2]}"/>
    <xacro:property name="lens_env_texture_size"    value="${lens_single_elements_seq[3]}"/>

    <xacro:property name="distortion_k_p_seq"       value="${distortion_k_p.split()}"/>

    <xacro:if value="${len(distortion_k_p_seq) != 5}">
      ${xacro.fatal("Invalid distortion_k_p parameter format. Expected format: 'k1 (R) k2 (R) k3 (R) p1 (R) p2 (R)'")}
    </xacro:if>

    <xacro:property name="k1"                       value="${distortion_k_p_seq[0]}"/>
    <xacro:property name="k2"                       value="${distortion_k_p_seq[1]}"/>
    <xacro:property name="k3"                       value="${distortion_k_p_seq[2]}"/>
    <xacro:property name="p1"                       value="${distortion_k_p_seq[3]}"/>
    <xacro:property name="p2"                       value="${distortion_k_p_seq[4]}"/>

    <xacro:property name="distortion_center_seq"    value="${distortion_center.split()}"/>

    <xacro:if value="${len(distortion_center_seq) != 2}">
      ${xacro.fatal("Invalid distortion_center parameter format. Expected format: 'center_x_norm (R) center_y_norm (R)'")}
    </xacro:if>

    <xacro:property name="center_norm"              value="${distortion_center_seq[0]} ${distortion_center_seq[1]}"/>

    <xacro:property name="gaussian_noise_seq"       value="${gaussian_noise.split()}"/>

    <xacro:if value="${len(gaussian_noise_seq) != 2}">
      ${xacro.fatal("Invalid gaussian_noise parameter format. Expected format: 'mean (R) stddev (R)'")}
    </xacro:if>

    <xacro:property name="gaussian_noise_mean"      value="${gaussian_noise_seq[0]}"/>
    <xacro:property name="gaussian_noise_stddev"    value="${gaussian_noise_seq[1]}"/>

    <save enabled="${save_enabled}">
      <path>${save_path}</path>
    </save>

    <triggered>${triggered}</triggered>

    <xacro:if value="${triggered}">
      <trigger_topic>${trigger_topic}</trigger_topic>
    </xacro:if>

    <camera_info_topic>${info_topic}</camera_info_topic>

    <visibility_mask>${visible_mask}</visibility_mask>

    <optical_frame_id>${optical_frame}</optical_frame_id>

    <image>
      <width>${image_width_px}</width>
      <height>${image_height_px}</height>
      <format>${image_format}</format>
      <anti_aliasing>${image_anti_aliasing}</anti_aliasing>
    </image>

    <clip>
      <near>${near_clip}</near>
      <far>${far_clip}</far>
    </clip>

    <distortion>
      <k1>${k1}</k1>
      <k2>${k2}</k2>
      <k3>${k3}</k3>
      <p1>${p1}</p1>
      <p2>${p2}</p2>
      <center>${center_norm}</center>
    </distortion>

    <lens>
      <type>${lens_type}</type>
      <scale_to_hfov>${lens_scale_to_hfov}</scale_to_hfov>
      <cutoff_angle>${radians(float(lens_cutoff_angle_deg))}</cutoff_angle>
      <env_texture_size>${lens_env_texture_size}</env_texture_size>

      <!--
      If lens_type is 'custom', use the 'lens_custom_function' parameter to define the 'custom_function' element.
      -->
      <xacro:if value="${lens_type == 'custom'}">
        <xacro:property name="lens_custom_function_seq" value="${lens_custom_function.split()}"/>

        <xacro:if value="${len(lens_custom_function_seq) != 5}">
          ${xacro.fatal("Invalid lens_custom_function parameter format. Expected format: 'c1 (R) c2 (R) c3 (R) f (R) fun (S)'")}
        </xacro:if>

        <xacro:property name="c1"  value="${lens_custom_function_seq[0]}"/>
        <xacro:property name="c2"  value="${lens_custom_function_seq[1]}"/>
        <xacro:property name="c3"  value="${lens_custom_function_seq[2]}"/>
        <xacro:property name="f"   value="${lens_custom_function_seq[3]}"/>
        <xacro:property name="fun" value="${lens_custom_function_seq[4]}"/>

        <custom_function>
          <c1>${c1}</c1>
          <c2>${c2}</c2>
          <c3>${c3}</c3>
          <f>${f}</f>
          <fun>${fun}</fun>
        </custom_function>
      </xacro:if>

      <!--
      According to the sdformat documentation, if lens_projection is defined, it overrides the intrinsics parameters
      fx, fy, cx, cy.
      Therefore, if lens_projection is defined, we use it.
      -->
      <xacro:if value="${lens_projection != ''}">
        <xacro:property name="lens_projection_seq" value="${lens_projection.split()}"/>

        <xacro:if value="${len(lens_projection_seq) != 6}">
          ${xacro.fatal("Invalid lens_projection parameter format. Expected format: 'p_fx p_fy p_cx p_cy tx ty'")}
        </xacro:if>

        <xacro:property name="p_fx" value="${lens_projection_seq[0]}"/>
        <xacro:property name="p_fy" value="${lens_projection_seq[1]}"/>
        <xacro:property name="p_cx" value="${lens_projection_seq[2]}"/>
        <xacro:property name="p_cy" value="${lens_projection_seq[3]}"/>
        <xacro:property name="tx"   value="${lens_projection_seq[4]}"/>
        <xacro:property name="ty"   value="${lens_projection_seq[5]}"/>

        <projection>
          <p_fx>${p_fx}</p_fx>
          <p_fy>${p_fy}</p_fy>
          <p_cx>${p_cx}</p_cx>
          <p_cy>${p_cy}</p_cy>
          <tx>${tx}</tx>
          <ty>${ty}</ty>
        </projection>

        <!--
        When 'lens_projection' is used, still set the 'lens_xy_skew' in the intrinsics element since there is no other
        way to set it.
        Since the rest of the element in intrinsics are required (see sdformat documentation), we must set them,
        otherwise sdf parser will raise an error. However, in practical terms, no matter the values we give to fx, fy,
        cx, cy here, they will be overridden by the values in projection (p_fx, p_fy, p_cx, p_cy), as we can read in the
        sdformat documentation.
        Therefore, we set the parameters fx, fy, cx, cy to p_fx, p_fy, p_cx, p_cy respectively, just to avoid errors
        in sdf parsing, knowing that these values will be overridden later by the projection values.
        -->
        <intrinsics>
          <fx>${p_fx}</fx>
          <fy>${p_fy}</fy>
          <cx>${p_cx}</cx>
          <cy>${p_cy}</cy>
          <s>${lens_xy_skew}</s>
        </intrinsics>
      </xacro:if>

      <!--
      If no custom function is defined and no lens_projection is defined, use lens_intrinsics to set the intrinsics
      element.
      -->
      <xacro:if value="${lens_projection == ''}">
        <xacro:property name="lens_intrinsics_seq" value="${lens_intrinsics.split()}"/>

        <xacro:if value="${len(lens_intrinsics_seq) != 4}">
          ${xacro.fatal("Invalid lens_intrinsics parameter format. Expected format: 'fx (R) fy (R) cx (R) cy (R)'")}
        </xacro:if>

        <xacro:property name="fx"  value="${lens_intrinsics_seq[0]}"/>
        <xacro:property name="fy"  value="${lens_intrinsics_seq[1]}"/>
        <xacro:property name="cx"  value="${lens_intrinsics_seq[2]}"/>
        <xacro:property name="cy"  value="${lens_intrinsics_seq[3]}"/>

        <intrinsics>
          <fx>${fx}</fx>
          <fy>${fy}</fy>
          <cx>${cx}</cx>
          <cy>${cy}</cy>
          <s>${lens_xy_skew}</s>
        </intrinsics>
      </xacro:if>
    </lens>

    <noise>
      <type>gaussian</type>
      <mean>${gaussian_noise_mean}</mean>
      <stddev>${gaussian_noise_stddev}</stddev>
    </noise>

  </xacro:macro>
</robot>
